WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.090
- [Narrator] Here, South
Korea's largest defense company

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.073
is testing a new batch of weapons.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.860
The Wall Street Journal got a rare look

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.570
inside Hanwha's facilities

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.340
as it constructs how it's has destined

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.050
for Poland and submarines

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.543
that could bolster NATO's
arsenal in the future.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.380
That says South Korea is
increasing its arms sales

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.830
at a rate faster than
anywhere else in the world.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.420
In just a five year period,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.980
it has grown its defense exports by 177%.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.210
Although the US, Russia

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.360
and France have considerably
larger defense industries

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.500
analysts say the pace

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.020
at which South Korea
is expanding its output

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.050
marks a substantial shift for the future.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.850
So here's how the country is transforming

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.070
into a defense giant and what it means

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.873
for the western military alliance.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.330
Workers inside this Hanwha
plant are building dozens

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.150
of self-propelled K9 Howitzers.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.370
- Hanwha is South Korea's
largest defense company.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:00.123
They are playing a big role
in South Korea's arms exports.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.880
- [Narrator] That's largely due to Poland,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.680
a key NATO member purchasing them

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.790
as part of a $13.7 billion arms deal.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.650
Military analysts say the rapid speed

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:14.400
the workers here can build
them is a major selling point.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.710
The process is completed in three stages.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.980
Assembling the skeleton of the
weapon, connecting the parts

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.380
and then testing its performance.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.670
Although the company won't disclose

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.290
specific production times,

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.063
recent deliveries have been prompt.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.170
The first shipment of K9s
arrived in Poland in December

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.813
just over three months
after the deal was signed.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.440
That's in contrast to
other arms deals in Europe

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.503
that are still ongoing years
after the agreements were made.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.120
Hanwha says a quick completion
time is a major part

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.041
of its strategy to entice new customers.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:50.700
(gun firing)

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.500
Military analysts say South Korea is able

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.520
to build these systems

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.070
faster than some western
counterparts in part

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.833
because of decades of
escalating regional tensions.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.850
At the end of the Cold War,
military powers in Europe

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.470
began reducing their capacity

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.750
to produce conventional
weapons while the threat

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.933
from North Korea led Seoul
to ramp its production.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.610
In the years since then, South
Korea has mostly focused on

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.040
developing traditional
battlefield weapons.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.040
- So while countries like
the US have focused on

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.880
creating high-end weapons such as aircraft

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.200
and nuclear submarines,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.610
South Korea has focused on
other weapons such as artillery.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.480
- [Narrator] But now it's looking

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.483
to export more advanced
naval designs to the west.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.320
At this Hanwha shipyard,
workers are building vessels

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.400
including the KSS-III, the
company's newest submarine.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.690
Industry analysts say it's one

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.840
of the most affordable pieces
of its caliber in the world.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.570
Hanwha is negotiating to
sell the diesel powered craft

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.000
to Canada, another NATO member.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.620
The company is trying to keep costs

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.680
and production time down
by using automated machines

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.777
and producing more components locally.

00:02:57.777 --> 00:03:00.835
(speaking in Korean)

00:03:00.835 --> 00:03:03.910
(speaking in Korean)

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.993
(speaking in Korean)

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.590
The company plans to double
its production capacity

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.530
for these systems by 2027.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.060
Military analysts say the designs

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.150
could help bolster NATO in
the future as China's navy,

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.003
which is the world's
largest, continues to expand.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.500
Traditionally, NATO countries buy weapons

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.180
from other member states.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.680
But South Korea, an ally

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.660
of the alliance has
been fulfilling a niche

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.489
for weapons that are in demand
due to the war in Ukraine.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:35.670
(gun firing)

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:36.840
- The US, for example,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.570
has also struggled with supply shortages,

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:44.280
especially as it continues to
send lethal aid to Ukraine.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.260
- [Narrator] Military analysts
say supply chain problems

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.270
haven't been an issue for South Korea

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.720
partly because it's not
sending lethal weapons to Kyiv.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.060
- Certainly we know that South Korea

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.900
has been facing pressure
from the US and also NATO

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.990
to provide more than
non-lethal aid to Ukraine

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:04.650
because many European countries
and the US are struggling

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.320
in trying to backfill their supply

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.600
and they know that South Korea is one

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.180
of the most technologically
advanced countries

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.473
with experience in exporting their arms.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.980
- [Narrator] For South Korea's government,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.283
expanding its defense exports
is a key policy objective.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.420
President Yoon Suk Yeol is
relying on companies like Hanwha

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.440
to reach the goal

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.833
of becoming the fourth
largest arms exporter by 2027.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.400
The rapid growth is not unsustainable

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.440
according to some military
analysts who point

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.383
to the country's long tradition
of manufactured exports.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.650
That includes everything from cars, phones

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.500
and semiconductors to container ships.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.410
However, as orders of
these weapons stack up

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.170
defense analysts say Seoul
may be over promising

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:53.763
how fast it can complete them.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.970
Being able to keep delivering them on time

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.376
will be key for its success.

00:04:59.376 --> 00:05:01.959
(gentle music)

